WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.760
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:12.640
In June 2020, OpenAI published an incredible&nbsp;
AI-based technique by the name Image-GPT.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:19.200
The problem here was simple to understand, but&nbsp;
nearly impossible to actually do: so here goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:26.000
we give it an incomplete image, and we ask&nbsp;
the AI to fill in the missing pixels. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.920
of course, an immensely difficult task, because&nbsp;
these images may depict any part of the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.760
around us. It would have to know a great deal&nbsp;
about our world to be able to continue the images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.520
so how well did it do? Let’s have a look!

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:49.760
This is undoubtedly a cat. But look! See that&nbsp;
white part that is just starting? The interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:57.920
part has been sneakily cut out of the image. What&nbsp;
could that be? A piece of paper? Something else?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:05.280
Now, let’s leave the dirty work to the machine and&nbsp;
ask it to finish it! Oh yeah, that makes sense.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:12.560
Now, even better, let’s have a look at this&nbsp;
water droplet example too. We humans, know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.240
since we see the remnants of ripples over there&nbsp;
too, there must be a splash, but the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:27.360
is - does the AI know that? Oh yes, yes it&nbsp;
does! Amazing! And the true image for reference.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:35.520
But wait a second. If Image GPT could understand&nbsp;
that this is a splash, and finish the image like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.800
this, then, here is an absolutely insane idea. If&nbsp;
a machine can understand that this is a splash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:50.080
could it, maybe, not only finish the&nbsp;
photo, but make a video out of it? Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:56.800
that is indeed an absolutely insane idea, we&nbsp;
like those around here. So, what do you think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.880
is this a reasonable question,&nbsp;
or is this still science fiction?

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.040
Well, let’s have a look at what this new&nbsp;
learning-based method does when looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:11.120
such an image. It would do something&nbsp;
very similar to what we would do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.360
look at the image, estimate the direction of&nbsp;
the motion, recognize that these ripples should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:23.920
probably travel outwards, and based on the fact&nbsp;
that we’ve seen many splashes in our lives, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:31.120
we had the artistic skill, we could surely fill in&nbsp;
something similar. So, can the machine do it too?

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.800
And now, hold on to your papers, because&nbsp;
this technique does exactly that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:46.000
Whoa! Please meet Eulerian Motion Synthesis.&nbsp;
And it not only works amazingly well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.880
but look at the output video. It even&nbsp;
loops perfectly. Yum yum yum, I love it!

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.440
And it works mostly on fluids and smoke.&nbsp;
I like that! I like that a lot, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:06.240
fluids and smoke have difficult, but predictable&nbsp;
motion. That is an excellent combination for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.720
especially given that you see plenty&nbsp;
of those simulations on this channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.280
so if you are a long-time Fellow Scholar,&nbsp;
you already have a keen eye for them.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.640
Here are a few example images, paired&nbsp;
with the synthesized motion fields,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.880
these define the trajectory of&nbsp;
each pixel, or in other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:31.840
regions that the AI thinks should be animated&nbsp;
and how it thinks should be animated.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.760
Now, it gets better, I have found three things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.320
that I did not expect to work, but was&nbsp;
pleasantly surprised that they did.&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:49.040
One, reflections, kind of work.
Two, fire. Kind of works.&nbsp;

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.600
And now, if you have been holding on to your&nbsp;
papers so far, now, squeeze that paper, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:03.680
here comes the best one, three, my beard works&nbsp;
too. Yes, you heard it right. Now, first things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:11.680
first, this is not any kind of beard, this is an&nbsp;
algorithmic beard that was made by an AI, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:18.080
it is animated as if it were a piece of&nbsp;
fluid using a different AI. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:23.440
this is not supposed to be a correct result,&nbsp;
just a happy accident, but in any case, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:29.440
sounds like something straight out of a science&nbsp;
fiction movie. I also like how this has a nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:36.400
Obi-Wan Kenobi quality to it. Loving it. Thank you&nbsp;
very much to my friend Oliver Wang and the authors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:42.800
for being so kind and generating these results&nbsp;
only for us. That is a huge honor, thank you.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:49.600
This previous work is from 2019 and creates&nbsp;
high-quality motion, but, has a limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:56.720
understanding of the scene itself. And of course,&nbsp;
let’s see how the new method fares in these cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.480
Oh yeah, this is a huge leap forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.720
And what I like even better here is that new&nbsp;
research techniques often provide different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.720
tradeoffs than previous methods, but are rarely&nbsp;
strictly better than them. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:18.880
competing techniques usually do some things better&nbsp;
and some things worse than their predecessors…but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:28.240
not this. Look, this is so much better across&nbsp;
the board. That is such a rare sight. Amazing.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.240
Now, of course, not even this technique is&nbsp;
perfect. For example, this part of the image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:41.360
should have been animated, but remains stationary.&nbsp;
Also, even though it did well with reflections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:48.000
refraction is a tougher nut to crack. Finally,&nbsp;
thin geometry also still remains a challenge.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:54.320
But this was one paper that made the impossible&nbsp;
possible, and just think about what we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:06:00.160
be able to do, two more papers down the&nbsp;
line. My goodness. What a time to be alive!

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.840
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

